Id: time001w
Severity: WARN
Category: services
Check: check_timesync
Fix: Turn one time daemon on: timedatectl set-ntp true (Linux), ntpd_enable in rc.conf (FreeBSD), automatic date and time (macOS).
References: systemd-timesyncd(8), chronyd(8); ntpd(8), rc.conf(5); systemsetup(8)
Controls: CIS 8.4; NIST AU-8, SC-45; ISO 8.17

No time synchronisation daemon is running: the check looked for the
platform's daemons (chronyd, systemd-timesyncd, ntpd, OpenNTPD or
ptp4l on Linux; ntpd, OpenNTPD or chronyd on FreeBSD; timed, ntpd or
chronyd on macOS) and found none keeping the clock. On FreeBSD
ntpdate alone counts as none: it sets the clock once, at boot, and
does not keep it. On macOS timed with automatic date and time off
counts as none too: it follows nothing. The clock then drifts, by
seconds a day on ordinary hardware and more in virtual machines, and
what depends on it goes wrong: logs from different machines no longer
line up in an investigation, TLS certificates look expired or not yet
valid, and Kerberos and TOTP codes are refused.

To fix it, turn on the daemon the system ships: 'timedatectl set-ntp
true' starts systemd-timesyncd on Linux, or install chrony (the
default on Fedora, RHEL and SUSE, and the better choice for a
server); ntpd_enable="YES" in rc.conf on FreeBSD; automatic date and
time (System Settings, or 'sudo systemsetup -setusingnetworktime on')
on macOS. A virtual machine whose hypervisor sets its clock, or a jail
on its host's clock, may not need one; if so, accept the finding with
tigris-accept.
